The essential shape virtualization libraries provide, simplified to show the core idea:

```javascript
function useVirtualRange(scrollTop, viewportHeight, itemHeights, overscan = 5) {
  // Find the first item whose bottom edge is below scrollTop
  const startIndex = findFirstVisible(itemHeights, scrollTop);
  const endIndex = findLastVisible(itemHeights, scrollTop + viewportHeight);

  return {
    renderStart: Math.max(0, startIndex - overscan),
    renderEnd: Math.min(itemHeights.length, endIndex + overscan),
    // Total height keeps the scrollbar accurate even though
    // most items aren't actually mounted in the DOM
    totalHeight: itemHeights.reduce((sum, h) => sum + h, 0),
    offsetTop: sumHeightsBefore(itemHeights, startIndex - overscan),
  };
}
```

Only `renderEnd - renderStart` `FeedItem`s ever mount at once — typically 15–30 regardless of whether the user has scrolled through 50 posts or 50,000. The `overscan` buffer exists so a fast scroll doesn't flash empty space while new items mount just outside the viewport.
